{
  "dom_id": "step-e7080",
  "name": "Example: Simplifying an Expression Using the Law that Disjunction Absorbs Conjunction",
  "problem": "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $C?$\n\n- $(C \\land B) \\lor C$\n\n- $B \\lor (B \\land C)$\n\n- $C \\lor (C \\lor B)$",
  "worked_solution": "The law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nC \\lor (C \\land B) \\equiv C.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is logically equivalent to $C.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(C\u2227B)\u2228C & \u2261C\u2228(C\u2227B) \\\\  & \u2261C.\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is *not* logically equivalent to $C.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B\u2228(B\u2227C) & \u2261B\u2262C.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is *not* logically equivalent to $C.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
